  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 2,
   "plaque": "arts_brian_boru_harp",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "The Brian Boru Harp",
   "text": "The oldest Irish harp to survive, made in the 1400s of willow and brass, is kept here in Trinity's Long Room. It was called Brian Boru's harp long after his death in 1014, which it never saw. Its outline is on the Irish coins, the passport and the Presidential seal, making Ireland the only country with a musical instrument for its national emblem, and Guinness has used it as a trademark since 1876, turned the other way to keep the lawyers happy. It has not been played in a very long time, and it is not about to start."
  }
